=== layout: subsection

# 22. Supplements and DSHEA

=== layout: one-panel

# 22. Questions (1 of 4)

@body

- Before DSHEA of 1994: How does the FDA define the difference between a dietary supplement and a pharmaceutical drug?
- In what ways did pre-1994 FDA regulations for dietary supplements mirror those for food additives?
- What specific safety and efficacy evaluations were required for dietary supplements before the 1994 DSHEA act?
- Can vitamin C prevent the common cold?
- What role did Linus Pauling play in the myth of vitamin C?
- Politics around the DSHEA of 1994: How did the media create a narrative of governmental overreach and interference with personal health freedoms?

=== layout: one-panel

# 22. Questions (2 of 4)

@body

- How did FDA Commissioner Dr. David Kessler's statements regarding dietary supplements get misinterpreted by the media and public?
- How did those statements contribute to the 'keep the government away from my vitamins' campaign?
- How did lobbying by the dietary supplement industry make it to where supplements would be regulated more like foods than drugs?
- How did public opinion and industry pressure overrule the role of scientific evidence?
- After DSHEA of 1994: Who bears the responsibility for proving the safety and efficacy of dietary supplements: the FDA or the manufacturer?

=== layout: one-panel

# 22. Questions (3 of 4)

@body

- And what is required for the FDA to demonstrate that a supplement is harmful?
- What is the role of the FDA in dietary supplements today?
- Why are dietary supplements permitted to state they 'support the immune system' but prohibited from claiming they 'prevent illness,' and what criteria does the FDA use to distinguish between permissible support claims and impermissible health claims?"

=== layout: one-panel

# 22. Questions (4 of 4)

@body

- Future of Supplement Industry: Can you identify some private regulatory bodies that oversee dietary supplements (NPA, Ul, USP, NSF International, ConsumerLab.com) and describe the types of certifications or assurances they provide to consumers?
- Why do we need private regulatory bodies?
- What recent high-profile cases have there been regarding supplement safety or false claims, e.g. Herbalife?

=== layout: one-panel

# 22. A Different Premarket System

@body

- DSHEA was enacted in 1994. Dietary supplements are regulated under a framework different from the drug-approval system.
- Manufacturers are responsible for complying with safety, labeling, and manufacturing requirements. FDA generally does not approve supplements for safety and effectiveness before sale.
- Some new dietary ingredients have notification requirements, and FDA can act against adulterated or misbranded products.
- The absence of drug-style premarket approval does not mean there are no rules.

@citation

https://www.fda.gov/food/information-consumers-using-dietary-supplements/questions-and-answers-dietary-supplements

=== layout: one-panel

# 22. What Changed Before and After 1994?

@body

- Before DSHEA, disputes concerned how\
  food, food-additive, and drug provisions applied to particular supplement ingredients and claims.
- It is inaccurate to say that all pre-1994 supplements had to prove drug-like efficacy before sale.
- The political campaign framed access as personal freedom and FDA action as interference. That framing should be compared with the actual law and evidence.
- Industry influence, consumer choice, and uncertainty about product quality are all legitimate topics for discussion.

@citation

https://www.fda.gov/food/information-consumers-using-dietary-supplements/questions-and-answers-dietary-supplements

=== layout: one-panel

# 22. Read the Claim, Not Just the Bottle

@body

- A statement about supporting normal body structure\
  or function is different from claiming to diagnose, treat, cure, or prevent a disease.
- Required disclaimers do not prove a claim is true. Manufacturers still need appropriate substantiation and truthful labeling.
- An ingredient can be natural and still interact with medicines or cause harm at a particular dose.
- Ask what was tested: the ingredient, this formulation, the actual batch, or a meaningful health outcome?

@citation

https://www.fda.gov/food/information-consumers-using-dietary-supplements/questions-and-answers-dietary-supplements

=== layout: one-panel

# 22. Vitamin C: Nutrition and Treatment

@body

- Vitamin C is essential; preventing deficiency is different from preventing the common cold.
- NIH's evidence summary does not support routine vitamin C supplementation to reduce cold incidence in the general population. Regular supplementation can modestly shorten duration,\
  with context-specific findings.
- Linus Pauling's advocacy helped popularize much stronger expectations. Fame and a plausible biochemical role do not establish a clinical outcome.
- Check the population, timing, dose, and endpoint before repeating a supplement claim.

@citation

https://ods.od.nih.gov/factsheets/VitaminC-HealthProfessional/

=== layout: one-panel

# 22. What a Quality Seal Can Tell You

@body

- Independent programs such as USP verification or appropriate NSF certification can assess defined aspects of identity, content, contaminants, manufacturing, or banned-substance screening.
- Read the scope of the actual program. A trade-association logo is not the same thing as independent product verification.
- Meeting a content or contamination standard does not demonstrate that the product treats a disease.
- Separate quality testing from evidence of effectiveness and from a seller's business practices.

@citation

https://www.fda.gov/food/information-consumers-using-dietary-supplements/questions-and-answers-dietary-supplements

=== layout: big-image

@image

![22. Read a Real Supplement Disclaimer](assets/set6_sources/topic_22_014.png)

@caption

Compare a structure/function claim with the adjacent disclaimer. Which claim would require evidence of disease treatment, and what does this label actually establish?

=== layout: big-image

@image

![22. What Does USP Verification Establish?](assets/set6_sources/topic_22_183.png)

@caption

A quality-verification mark has a defined scope. Identity, amount, contaminants, and manufacturing quality are different claims from prevention or treatment of a disease.

=== layout: one-panel

# 22. An Enforcement Case Has a Scope

@body

- The FTC's 2016 Herbalife settlement concerned alleged deception about earnings and the structure of its multilevel-marketing operations.
- The agreement provided $200 million in consumer redress and required changes in compensation and retail-sales verification.
- That is a business-practices case, not a clinical trial proving a supplement works or a finding that every product contains a particular contaminant.
- For any headline, identify the agency, allegation, evidence, remedy, and limits of what was decided.

@citation

https://www.ftc.gov/system/files/documents/public_statements/971213/160715herbalifestatement.pdf

=== layout: subsection

# 23. Generic Drug Regulation

=== layout: one-panel

# 23. Questions (1 of 3)

@body

- In what ways does the Hatch-Waxman Act affect the production and regulation of generic drugs?
- How does the Hatch-Waxman Act strike a balance between extending patent life for brand-name drugs and encouraging generic drug competition?
- How do pricing strategies differ between generic and brand-name drug manufacturers?
- What is the role of market exclusivity periods in the Hatch-Waxman Act, and how do they impact the entry of generic drugs into the market?
- In what ways have consumers benefited from the Hatch-Waxman Act economically?

=== layout: one-panel

# 23. Questions (2 of 3)

@body

- How have patent litigations under the Hatch-Waxman Act influenced the pharmaceutical landscape?
- What are some significant legal precedents set by Hatch-Waxman-related court cases that have shaped the generic drug industry?
- How has the introduction of generic drugs under the Abbreviated New Drug Application (ANDA) influenced healthcare costs?
- What are the requirements for an ANDA submission, and how do they ensure that generic drugs are safe and effective?
- What are the scientific and regulatory challenges in establishing bioequivalence for complex drug products?

=== layout: one-panel

# 23. Questions (3 of 3)

@body

- How does the FDA address variations in bioequivalence standards for drugs with narrow therapeutic indices?
- How does the FDA ensure that generic drugs continue to meet safety and efficacy standards post-approval?

=== layout: one-panel

# 23. An Abbreviated Application

@body

- A generic drug must meet the applicable requirements for sameness to its reference product, including active ingredient, strength, dosage form, route, and bioequivalence.
- An ANDA relies in part on the reference product's established safety and effectiveness rather than repeating its full development program.
- Manufacturing quality and product performance still matter.
- Biosimilars use a different pathway for biologics; the small-molecule generic model does not transfer unchanged to complex biological products.

@citation

https://www.fda.gov/drugs/generic-drugs/generic-drug-facts

=== layout: one-panel

# 23. Bioequivalence Is About Exposure

@body

- Bioequivalence studies commonly compare pharmacokinetic measures such as AUC and maximum concentration under an appropriate design.
- For many immediate-release products, the 90 percent confidence interval for the test/reference geometric-mean ratio must fall within 80-125 percent for specified measures.
- This is not permission to put 80 percent of the labeled active ingredient in every generic tablet.
- Specific products and study designs can have additional or different requirements.

@citation

https://www.fda.gov/media/163638/download

=== layout: one-panel

# 23. What Can Differ?

@body

- Inactive ingredients, appearance, or packaging may differ within applicable requirements; active ingredient and required performance cannot simply be changed at will.
- Patent expiration and regulatory exclusivity affect entry, while competition can lower prices.
- Neither a brand name nor a low price substitutes for quality evidence.
- Ask whether a concern involves identity, exposure, impurities, dissolution, manufacturing records, or the clinical use of a particular product.

@citation

https://www.fda.gov/drugs/generic-drugs/generic-drug-facts

=== layout: big-image

@image

![23. An Interval, Not an Ingredient Allowance](assets/set6_drawings/bioequivalence.drawn.svg)

@caption

Illustrative invented exposure-ratio intervals. Both boundaries of the applicable confidence interval must meet the study criterion.

=== layout: one-panel

# 23. The Hatch-Waxman Tradeoff

@body

- The 1984 Hatch-Waxman framework helped establish the modern abbreviated pathway for generic drugs and provided mechanisms for patent-term restoration for qualifying products.
- The balance concerns incentives to develop new medicines and access to lower-cost competitors without repeating unnecessary studies.
- Patent protection and regulatory exclusivity are separate rights that can affect when a competitor enters.
- Ask which period of protection applies, what the generic applicant must demonstrate, and what the public receives in return.

@citation

https://www.fda.gov/about-fda/fda-history/milestones-us-food-and-drug-law

=== layout: subsection

# 24. International Generic Drug Quality

=== layout: one-panel

# 24. Questions (1 of 3)

@body

- Quality Control Concerns: What factors lead to quality control problems in generic drug manufacturing, especially overseas?
- How do manufacturing lapses impact the safety and effectiveness of generic drugs?
- How do economic and political pressures affect the oversight and quality assurance of generic drug production?
- Regulatory Challenges: What obstacles do organizations like the FDA face when monitoring international pharmaceutical manufacturers?
- What are the specific challenges associated with conducting surprise inspections at these international facilities?

=== layout: one-panel

# 24. Questions (2 of 3)

@body

- How does the manipulation of data and fraudulent reporting endanger drug safety?
- Can you identify cases of corporate misconduct in generic drug production and their consequences?
- What methods can regulators use to uncover and deter test result falsification by drug companies?
- Issues of Inefficacy: How does cost reduction in production affect the trustworthiness of generic drugs?
- Can you provide instances where substandard generics have led to treatment failures?

=== layout: one-panel

# 24. Questions (3 of 3)

@body

- What is the impact of ineffective (less active ingredient than labeled) or contaminated generics on patient health and public trust in the pharmaceutical industry?
- Consumer Knowledge: What approaches can consumers take to better understand the origins and quality of their medications?
- How can medical professionals promote higher standards in drug manufacturing?
- I recommend the book "Bottle of Lies: The Inside Story of the Generic Drug Boom" by Katherine Eban as it goes deeper into these topics above.

=== layout: one-panel

# 24. The Standard Travels with the Market

@body

- Drugs sold in the United States must meet U.S. requirements whether made domestically or abroad.
- Manufacturing and testing may span several sites and suppliers. The finished label alone may not show the whole supply chain.
- FDA uses inspections and other oversight tools, including actions affecting imports.
- Country of manufacture is not a substitute for evidence about a particular facility, product, or batch.

@citation

https://www.justice.gov/archives/opa/pr/generic-drug-manufacturer-ranbaxy-pleads-guilty-and-agrees-pay-500-million-resolve-false

=== layout: one-panel

# 24. Data Integrity Protects Safety

@body

- A falsified test result removes the evidence that a process is under control, even when a tablet looks normal.
- Missing original records, selective reporting, and repeating tests until a passing result appears can conceal real quality problems.
- The 2013 Ranbaxy criminal and civil resolution provides a documented example involving manufacturing violations and false statements.
- Use documented cases to examine incentives and oversight; do not infer that every foreign generic is defective.

@citation

https://www.justice.gov/archives/opa/pr/generic-drug-manufacturer-ranbaxy-pleads-guilty-and-agrees-pay-500-million-resolve-false

=== layout: one-panel

# 24. Investigate Bottle of Lies

@body

- The instructor recommends Katherine Eban's Bottle of Lies to explore failures in the generic-drug industry.
- Ask what evidence establishes the particular failure, who knew about it, and what prevented or delayed correction.
- Patients and clinicians can consult FDA recalls, safety communications, and product information; appearance or price cannot establish batch quality.
- Reliable sourcing, truthful records, independent verification, and enforceable accountability are system-level questions.

@citation

https://www.justice.gov/archives/opa/pr/generic-drug-manufacturer-ranbaxy-pleads-guilty-and-agrees-pay-500-million-resolve-false

=== layout: subsection

# 25. PFAS: Forever Chemicals

=== layout: one-panel

# 25. Questions (1 of 2)

@body

- What are PFAS, and why are they termed "forever chemicals"?
- What are common applications of PFAS?
- What products are they commonly used in?
- For the following items, describe how PFAS exposure can impact human health: drinking water, dental floss, food packaging, nonstick cookware, stain-resistant carpets, and waterproof clothing. How do consumer choices affect the presence of PFAS in products?
- What health risks are tied to PFAS exposure?
- How much PFAS does the average American have in their bodies?

=== layout: one-panel

# 25. Questions (2 of 2)

@body

- How is the EPA addressing PFAS concerns?
- Which PFAS are banned and for what reason?
- How are environmental PFAS levels monitored and managed?
- What challenges exist in decontaminating PFAS-polluted sites?
- Are there any recent scientific breakthroughs help in degrading or neutralizing PFAS?
- The film "Dark Waters" (2019) provides a dramatic portrayal of issues related to PFAS.

=== layout: one-panel

# 25. A Family, Not One Chemical

@body

- PFAS means per- and polyfluoroalkyl substances: a large and varied family of fluorinated chemicals.
- Some have been used for water, oil, heat, or stain resistance in products and industrial processes.
- Persistence, mobility, accumulation, and toxicity differ among compounds. Finding one property does not establish every other property for the whole family.
- Exposure assessment needs the chemical, concentration, route, duration, and population.

@citation

https://www.epa.gov/dwreginfo/pfas-rule-implementation

=== layout: one-panel

# 25. Trace Exposure to the Measurement

@body

- Contaminated drinking water and food can contribute to exposure; consumer products and occupational activities can also matter.
- Blood surveys detect several PFAS in much of the population, but there is no single meaningful average amount for all PFAS in every American.
- Report the measured compound, units, population, sampling year, and distribution.
- Evidence links some well-studied PFAS to outcomes including immune-response changes and certain cancers; distinguish the compound and the strength of the evidence.

@citation

https://www.epa.gov/dwreginfo/pfas-rule-implementation

=== layout: one-panel

# 25. Water Limits and Product Bans

@body

- EPA's 2024 drinking-water rule established enforceable limits, including 4 parts per trillion each for PFOA and PFOS.
- EPA's May 2026 actions propose changes for other covered PFAS and a mechanism for certain compliance extensions. A proposal is not a final repeal.
- Product restrictions, water standards, waste rules, and contamination cleanup are different controls.
- Status checked for November 12, 2026: name the specific rule and whether a change is proposed or effective.

@citation

https://www.epa.gov/dwreginfo/pfas-rule-implementation

=== layout: one-panel

# 25. Capture Is Not Destruction

@body

- Activated carbon, ion exchange, or membranes can remove selected PFAS from water under suitable conditions.
- That creates a concentrated stream or spent material that still needs management.
- Destruction research must show what happened to carbon-fluorine bonds, fluorine, and transformation products, including shorter-chain compounds.
- Dark Waters is the instructor's film recommendation. Use it to motivate questions about evidence, exposure, accountability, and the time between discovering harm and responding.

@citation

https://www.epa.gov/dwreginfo/pfas-rule-implementation

=== layout: big-image

@image

![25. Where Did the PFAS Go?](assets/set6_drawings/pfas_fate.drawn.svg)

@caption

Measure the outgoing water and the retained material. Removal from water alone does not demonstrate destruction.

=== layout: big-image

@image

![25. Dark Waters: Follow the Evidence](assets/set6_sources/topic_25_115.png)

@caption

The instructor recommends Dark Waters (2019). Ask who measured exposure, who controlled the records, and what it took to connect a local observation to a public response.

